  <h2>What is LineageOS?</h2>
  <p>
    LineageOS is a free, open-source operating system for phones and tablets,
    built on the Android Open Source Project (<a href="/AOSP">AOSP</a>). It is
    the community successor to CyanogenMod, the original mass-market custom
    ROM. Official builds are per-device, and each supported device has its own
    installation guide on the LineageOS wiki.
  </p>

  <h2>Why people install it</h2>
  <p>
    The three classic reasons: keeping a phone on current Android releases long
    after its maker has stopped shipping updates; a cleaner system without
    manufacturer or carrier bloatware; and extra privacy and customization
    controls built into the OS itself.
  </p>

  <h2>LineageOS and root</h2>
  <p>
    LineageOS does not ship with root access. Rooting a device running
    LineageOS follows the same paths as on stock Android — most commonly
    <a href="/Magisk">Magisk</a> or a kernel-based method such as
    <a href="/KernelSU">KernelSU</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you flash</h2>
  <p>
    Installing a custom OS requires an unlocked bootloader, and the process
    typically erases all data on the device — back up anything you want to
    keep first. Use only official builds and the device-specific instructions
    on lineageos.org; a build made for a different model can leave the device
    unbootable. Knowing your exact device and bootloader state is step one —
    our free <a href="/scan">browser-based scan</a> reports both over USB.
  </p>
